 belledby="Economics_of_Mixed_Health_Systems_27_May_2026-2" class="accConte
 nt" role="tabpanel" style="display: none\;">\n<div class="accContent__inne
 r">\n<div class="accContent__wrapper">\n<div id="component_3981186">\n<h3>
 Date and time</h3><p>Wednesday 27 May 2026\, 9.30am – 5pm</p><h3>Locatio
 n</h3><p>Monash College\, 750 Collins St\, Docklands VIC 3008</p><h3>Overv
 iew</h3><p>The Economics of Mixed Systems short course introduces particip
 ants to the economic principles underlying Australia's complex public-priv
 ate health care system. Topics include understanding the role of private h
 ealth insurance\, consumer demand for public and private care\, waiting ti
 me dynamics\, supply-side factors such as physician dual practice and crea
 m-skimming\, and contemporary issues facing the sector.</p><h3>Learning ou
 tcomes</h3><p>At the end of the course\, participants will be able to:</p>
 <ul><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Understand how economi
 sts conceptualise demand for public and private health care\, including th
 e costs of waiting.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Ev
 aluate the rationale for—and effectiveness of—private health insurance
  incentives.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Analyse s
 upply-side dynamics including dual practice\, co-location\, and cream-skim
 ming behaviour.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Apply 
 economic thinking to assess intended and unintended effects of policy inte
 rventions.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Critically 
 assess claims about the role of private health in relieving public system 
 pressures.</li></ul><h3>Who should attend?</h3><p>The course is designed f
 or those seeking to apply economic concepts and evidence to current health
  policy issues. No prior economics knowledge is required\; material is pre
 sented in a non-technical way with opportunities for group discussion.</p>
 <p>Suitable for:</p><ul><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Pu
 blic servants and health policy analysts.</li><li aria-level="1" style="li
 st-style-type:disc">Health service managers across public and private sect
 ors.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Health profession
 als.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Consultants in he
 alth and human services.</li></ul><h3>Cost</h3><ul><li>$950AUD for a singl
 e course\, or $1800AUD for the two-course package. <ul><li><strong>Volume 
 discounts are also available for more than three participants</strong> fro
 m the same organisation. Please <a href="https://www.monash.edu/business/c
 he/contact-us">contact us</a> directly for further information about how t
 o take advantage of the volume discounts.</li></ul><p>Fees include attenda
 nce at a full day of face-to-face seminars and group work\, catered lunch\
 , morning and afternoon tea.</p></li></ul><h3>Teaching staff</h3><h4><a hr
 ef="https://research.monash.edu/en/persons/terence-cheng"><img alt="Terenc
 e Cheng" height="175" src="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/image/0009
 /3888360/sq_Terence_Cheng.jpg" style="float: right\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1
 em\;" width="175"/>Associate Professor Terence Cheng</a>\, Theme Lead (Hea
 lth Financing and Market Organisation)\, Centre for Health Economics\, Mon
 ash Business School</h4><p>A/Prof Cheng is an expert on the economics of p
 ublic and private health systems. He has published extensively in leading 
 international journals on topics covering public and private healthcare an
 d insurance\, medical labour markets\, international markets for healthcar
 e\, and health and well-being. He is currently on the editorial board of t
 he international journal <em>Health Systems and Reform</em>.</p><p>A/Prof 
 Cheng has held academic and research appointments at universities in Austr
 alia\, Singapore and the United States. He returned to Australia in 2024 a
 fter a three-year stint as a Research Scientist of Health Systems Research
  and Policy at the Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health. He has consu
 lted for the World Health Organisation\, the World Bank and various govern
 ment departments in Australia.</p><h3>Enrolments</h3><p><a class="btn" hre
 f="https://events.humanitix.com/centre-for-health-economics-short-courses-
 26-and-27-may" target="_blank">Enrol now</a></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n
