 , 750 Collins St\, Docklands VIC 3008\n\nOverview\n\nThe Economics of Mixe
 d Systems short course introduces participants to the economic principles 
 underlying Australia's complex public-private health care system. Topics i
 nclude understanding the role of private health insurance\, consumer deman
 d for public and private care\, waiting time dynamics\, supply-side factor
 s such as physician dual practice and cream-skimming\, and contemporary is
 sues facing the sector.\n\nLearning outcomes\n\nAt the end of the course\,
  participants will be able to:\n\nWho should attend?\n\nThe course is desi
 gned for those seeking to apply economic concepts and evidence to current 
 health policy issues. No prior economics knowledge is required\; material 
 is presented in a non-technical way with opportunities for group discussio
 n.\n\nSuitable for:\n\nCost\n\nFees include attendance at a full day of fa
 ce-to-face seminars and group work\, catered lunch\, morning and afternoon
  tea.\n\nTeaching staff\n\nA/Prof Cheng is an expert on the economics of p
 ublic and private health systems. He has published extensively in leading 
 international journals on topics covering public and private healthcare an
 d insurance\, medical labour markets\, international markets for healthcar
 e\, and health and well-being. He is currently on the editorial board of t
 he international journal <em>Health Systems and Reform</em>.\n\nA/Prof Che
 ng has held academic and research appointments at universities in Australi
 a\, Singapore and the United States. He returned to Australia in 2024 afte
 r a three-year stint as a Research Scientist of Health Systems Research an
 d Policy at the Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health. He has consulte
 d for the World Health Organisation\, the World Bank and various governmen
 t departments in Australia.\n\nEnrolments\n\n<a class="btn" href="https://
